module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Filtration
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Noetherian.Defs
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Finiteness.Defs
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Module.LocalizedModule.Basic

/-!
# Localization of intersections of powers

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma Tag 00IQ, part (1)
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-krull-intersection-localization)

## Main statements

The intersection of the powers of an ideal in a finite module vanishes after
localizing away from every prime containing that ideal.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace RingTheory

/--
Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma Tag 00IQ, part (1): `N_f = 0` for the
localization of `N` away from `f`
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-krull-intersection-localization).

Proof, following Stacks Tag 00IQ (1) and the five blueprint steps:

1. The intersection `N` is a submodule of the finite module `M` over the Noetherian
   ring `R`, hence is finitely generated by
   `lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-submodule-finite` (Stacks Tag 00IK).
2. Localize at `p`, using `def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime` and
   `def-weil-pbc-module-localization`. The ring is Noetherian by
   `lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian`, and the images of generators of `M`
   generate its localization. The ideal `I R_p` lies in the maximal ideal,
   hence in the Jacobson radical of this local ring. Apply
   `lem-weil-pbc-krull-intersection-jacobson` (the local case is Stacks Tag 00IP)
   to obtain that the intersection of its powers times `M_p` is zero.
3. For each `x` in `N`, its image lies in `(I R_p)^n M_p` for every `n`, since
   localization carries sums of products from `I^n M` into that submodule.
   Its image is therefore zero. The kernel criterion in
   `def-weil-pbc-module-localization` gives a scalar outside `p` killing `x`.
4. Apply `lem-weil-pbc-finite-module-localization-zero-annihilator` to the finite
   module `N`: the product of scalars killing its finitely many generators
   is a single `f` outside `p` killing all of `N`.
5. By `def-weil-pbc-module-localization-away`, every fraction `x/f^e` of
   `N_f = LocalizedModule.Away f N` is zero, because `f` kills `x`; so `N_f`
   is the zero module, stated here as `Subsingleton (LocalizedModule.Away f N)`.
-/
theorem krull_intersection_localization
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (I : Ideal R) (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
    [Module.Finite R M]
    (p : Ideal R) [p.IsPrime] (hIp : I ≤ p) :
    let N : Submodule R M := ⨅ n : ℕ, I ^ n • ⊤
    ∃ f : R, f ∉ p ∧ Subsingleton (LocalizedModule.Away f N) := by
  sorry

end RingTheory
